["<aside class=\"callout why\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>The coast rut peaks in late November, inside your season. Bucks that hid all fall start moving in daylight. That is your best window.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 325\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"September to January. Bow only bucks September 1 to 9. Rifle bucks September 10 to December 15 in MUs 2-8, 2-18 and 2-19. Rut in November and early December, with peak breeding November 15 to December 5. Antlers drop by January. The peak falls inside the season.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"325\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Rut and season, side by side</text>\n<line x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"262\" stroke=\"#c9d3c0\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"64.0\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Sep</text><line x1=\"100.58823529411765\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"100.58823529411765\" y2=\"262\" stroke=\"#c9d3c0\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"134.58823529411765\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Oct</text><line x1=\"173.52941176470588\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"173.52941176470588\" y2=\"262\" stroke=\"#c9d3c0\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"207.52941176470588\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Nov</text><line x1=\"244.11764705882354\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"244.11764705882354\" y2=\"262\" stroke=\"#c9d3c0\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"278.11764705882354\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Dec</text><line x1=\"317.05882352941177\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"317.05882352941177\" y2=\"262\" stroke=\"#c9d3c0\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"351.05882352941177\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Jan</text><rect x=\"30.0\" y=\"74\" width=\"21.17647058823529\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Bow only bucks, Sep 1 to 9</text><rect x=\"51.17647058823529\" y=\"112\" width=\"228.23529411764702\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"143\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Rifle bucks, Sep 10 to Dec 15</text><rect x=\"173.52941176470588\" y=\"150\" width=\"91.7647058823529\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"388\" y=\"181\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Rut, Nov and early Dec</text><rect x=\"206.47058823529412\" y=\"188\" width=\"49.41176470588235\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#FF6A13\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"388\" y=\"219\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Peak breeding, Nov 15 to Dec 5</text><rect x=\"300.5882352941176\" y=\"226\" width=\"89.41176470588238\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#c9d3c0\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"257\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">Antlers drop, by Jan</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">MUs 2-8, 2-18, 2-19 (99%, synopsis p. 28)</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">The peak falls inside the season</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Coast deer breed in November and early December <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Most does conceive 15 November to 5 December <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: timing and the season</summary><aside class=\"callout warn\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><ul><li>\"Vancouver Island blacktails breed during November and early December, with most does conceiving during their second ovulation, from November 15 to December 5\" (90%, Nyberg and Janz 1990, citing Thomas 1970).</li><li>Thomas (1970, University of British Columbia thesis, 444 does from Vancouver Island): 8 or 9 day follicular cycles in the November to early December breeding season (80%, abstract via search preview).</li><li>BC deer account: rutting season \"in November and early December\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>WDFW (Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife): the rut \"normally occurs in November and December\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>MUs (Management Units) 2-8, 2-18, 2-19: bucks 10 September to 15 December; bow only bucks 1 to 9 September (99%, page 28).</li><li>Harrison is not Vancouver Island. Exact local peak dates: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. Treat late November as the likely peak (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 342\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Buck behaviour before and in the rut. 1 circles a rival with deliberate, stiff steps. 2 back arched, head low, tail flicking. 3 thrashes bushes with the antlers. 4 young bucks spar: bonding, not fighting. 5 in the rut, equal bucks fight head to head.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"342\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Read a buck before the rut</text>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"46\" width=\"372\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"69\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">1</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Circles a rival</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">deliberate, stiff steps</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"100\" width=\"372\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"123\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">2</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Back arched, head low</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"139\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">tail flicking</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"154\" width=\"372\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"177\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">3</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"174\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Thrashes bushes</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"193\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">with the antlers</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"208\" width=\"372\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"231\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">4</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"228\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Young bucks spar</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"247\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">bonding, not fighting</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"262\" width=\"372\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#FF6A13\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"285\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#FF6A13\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">5</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"282\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Rut: equal bucks fight</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"301\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">head to head</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"330\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">BC deer account (90%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Bucks circle rivals: stiff steps, back arched, head low, tail flicking <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>They thrash bushes with their antlers <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: displays and fights</summary><ul><li>In the weeks before the rut, bucks show more dominance and indirect threats (90%, BC deer account).</li><li>A dominant buck circles a rival with deliberate steps, back arched, head low, tail flicking. The smaller buck often bolts <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Bucks thrash bushes with their antlers <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Look for shredded saplings and stripped bark (Tip).</li><li>Young bucks of unequal size spar a lot. It is bonding, not fighting <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>In the rut, mature bucks of equal size fight head to head <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Rubs, scrapes and the rut in general: <a href=\"#/s/the-rut\">The rut</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 265\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Tending bond timeline: the buck follows a doe for 2 to 3 days before her heat, the heat lasts about a day, then he stays 3 to 4 days. If not bred she cycles again in 22 to 29 days. One buck is unlikely to breed more than 3 or 4 does in 28 days.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"265\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">How a buck tends a doe</text>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"90\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"90.0\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Follows her</text><text x=\"90.0\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">2 to 3 days</text><rect x=\"160\" y=\"90\" width=\"70\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#FF6A13\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"195.0\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Heat</text><text x=\"195.0\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">about 1 day</text><rect x=\"230\" y=\"90\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"305.0\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Stays with her</text><text x=\"305.0\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#17201A\">3 to 4 days</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">One buck, one doe</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Not bred? She cycles again</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">in 22 to 29 days (90%)</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">One buck: 3 or 4 does in 28 days</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>A buck follows a doe 2 to 3 days before her heat <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>She is receptive about a day <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: how breeding works</summary><ul><li>A doe is receptive for a short time, about a day. If not bred, she cycles again, usually 22 to 29 days later (90%, Nyberg and Janz, citing Wallmo 1978, Thomas and Cowan 1975).</li><li>A buck may follow a doe 2 or 3 days before her heat, then stay with her 3 or 4 days after mating <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>So one buck is not likely to breed more than 3 or 4 does in a 28 day period <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Tending bond\">Tending bond</button>: the buck keeps other bucks away until she mates or another buck pushes him off (90%, BC deer account).</li><li>Older, dominant bucks do most of the breeding, though yearlings can breed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>What it means for you: find the does and you find the bucks in late November (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-buck-velvet.jpg\" alt=\"Velvet in summer: antlers still growing.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Velvet in summer: antlers still growing. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Charles J. Sharp, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columbian_black-tailed_deer_(Odocoileus_hemionus_columbianus)_male_Mt_Rainier.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bt-buck.jpg\" alt=\"Hard antlers in fall, with a doe close by.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Hard antlers in fall, with a doe close by. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Philip Cohen, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columbian_Black-tailed_Deer_buck.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Heaviest about mid November to early December <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Antlers usually shed by January; regrow in summer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the body year</summary><ul><li>Columbian black tails are heaviest about the time of the rut, mid November to early December; through most winters adults lose weight, often 20 to 25% of their peak fall weight (90%, Nyberg and Janz).</li><li>Antlers are usually shed by January and regrow during summer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. BC deer account: dropped January to March in BC, older bucks first; regrown April to August <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Fawns are born after about 200 days, mostly in the first half of June <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>After the rut bucks are thin, some hurt, and they hide and rest (90%, BC deer account).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 325\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A day curve: deer movement peaks near dawn and dusk, lower at midday and at night, but deer can move any time. Legal hunting hours run from 1 hour before sunrise to 1 hour after sunset.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"325\" fill=\"#EEF1EA\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">The deer day</text>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"60\" width=\"372\" height=\"120\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M24 160 C60 160 70 80 96 80 C122 80 132 150 170 150 C220 150 230 150 260 150 C290 150 300 80 322 80 C346 80 356 160 376 160\" fill=\"#cfe0c3\" stroke=\"#1F3D2B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">dawn</text><text x=\"322\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">dusk</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">night</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">midday</text><text x=\"376\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">night</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"214\" width=\"300\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#FF6A13\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"210\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Legal hours</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">1 hour before sunrise to</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"282\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"normal\" fill=\"#17201A\">1 hour after sunset (99%)</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1F3D2B\">Most active near dawn and dusk (80%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Dawn and dusk still matter most <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Rut weeks: stay out through midday if you can (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: a rut weekend plan (all Tips)</summary><ul><li>Before: find does on cutblock edges at dusk in early November. Note where (Tip).</li><li>Dawn: sit or still hunt the edge where does fed (Tip).</li><li>Midday: still hunt slowly through cover between feeding and bedding areas; bucks cruise for does (Tip).</li><li>Dusk: back to the edge (Tip).</li><li>A doe that keeps looking back: wait. A buck may be behind her (Tip).</li><li>Region 2 deer: bucks only in your MUs. Never shoot the doe (99%, page 28).</li><li>Rattling and calls: no black tail specific study found. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> before relying on them.</li></ul></details><aside class=\"callout rule\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law): \"In the rut, the doe is the map. Watch her, then look behind her.\"</p></aside>","<aside class=\"callout mistake\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Hunting only the first weekend of the season and missing the November rut.</li><li>Shooting the first deer out after a doe without checking for antlers.</li><li>Walking fast through a doe area and pushing the buck out ahead.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>On your November weekend: at every doe you see, stop, wait 5 minutes and glass behind her.</p></aside>","<ul><li>Nyberg and Janz 1990, page 36 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. BC deer account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. WDFW <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Thomas 1970 abstract <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Synopsis page 28 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>. Checked 2026-10-23.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: every source and link</summary><ul><li>Nyberg and Janz, editors (1990), <a href=\"https://www.for.gov.bc.ca/hfd/pubs/docs/srs/Srs05.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\"Deer and Elk Habitats in Coastal Forests of Southern British Columbia\"</a>, page 36 (life history), read directly from page images 2026-10-23 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Thomas, D.C. (1970), \"Ovary, reproduction, and productivity of female Columbian black-tailed deer\", PhD thesis, University of British Columbia, <a href=\"https://open.library.ubc.ca/collections/831/items/1.0103943\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UBC Open Collections</a>, abstract via search preview 2026-10-23 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>BC Ministry of Environment, Lands and Parks (2000), <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/muledeer.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\"Mule and Black-tailed Deer in British Columbia\"</a>, read directly <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife, <a href=\"https://wdfw.wa.gov/species-habitats/living/species-facts/deer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Living with wildlife: deer</a>, read 2026-10-23 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 28, via <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-2\">Rule Book: Region 2</a> <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul></details>"]